🔹OSI (Open Systems Interconnection) Model [7 layers] 
💡 Each layer has a specific function:

1️⃣ Physical Layer – Wires, cables, Wi-Fi signals (hardware transmission)
2️⃣ Data Link Layer – MAC addresses, Ethernet, switching
3️⃣ Network Layer – IP addresses, routing (IPv4/IPv6)
4️⃣ Transport Layer – TCP, UDP (ensuring reliable/unreliable data transfer)
5️⃣ Session Layer – Manages sessions between applications
6️⃣ Presentation Layer – Encryption, data formatting (SSL/TLS, ASCII)
7️⃣ Application Layer – User interfaces & protocols (HTTP, DNS, FTP, SMTP)

🔹 TCP/IP Model (4 Layers)
A simplified version of the OSI model, which is more practical for real-world networking:

1️⃣ Network Interface – Physical transmission (Ethernet, Wi-Fi)
2️⃣ Internet Layer – Routing with IP addresses (IPv4, IPv6)
3️⃣ Transport Layer – Communication reliability (TCP, UDP)
4️⃣ Application Layer – End-user protocols (HTTP, FTP, SSH, DNS)

💡 Think of it like this:
🛣 IP Address = Your home address (changes based on location)
🏷 MAC Address = Your passport (remains constant)

🔹 Important Networking Protocols
✅ TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) – Reliable, ordered data delivery
✅ UDP (User Datagram Protocol) – Faster, connectionless, no guarantee of delivery
✅ HTTP/HTTPS – Web browsing & secure communication
✅ DNS (Domain Name System) – Resolves domain names to IPs
✅ SSH (Secure Shell) – Secure remote login

👉 Why does this matter in DevOps?
📌 DevOps involves CI/CD, cloud computing, automation, and security—all of which rely on strong networking knowledge!
